# Known seat names — the branch prefixes `wake_filter` may attribute to a runner (quince#265).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `forge-watch`'s ownership filter decides whether an event is this session's by
# prefix-matching the branch against declared runner names, and it read only the LOCAL registry. But
# the branch namespace is GLOBAL: the architect's `arch1/…` branch is correctly prefixed under the
# convention and still unattributable on the implementer box, because `arch1` was declared on the
# other box. So it failed open and woke every watch here.
#
# Measured 2026-07-30, and the arch box is worse than the implementer box rather than symmetric:
#
#   implementer box   declares r1 r2 r3 r4   suppresses 5 PRs
#   architect box     declares arch1 only    suppresses 0 — `other_runner_names` is empty, so the
#                                            filter is a documented no-op and has NEVER suppressed
#                                            anything on that seat
#
# THIS LIST IS AUTHORITATIVE, NOT ADVISORY, and that is the whole design (ruled on quince#265).
# `forge-watch runner set <name>` REFUSES a name that is absent here. A committed list nobody is
# forced to update is the drift class quince#200 and quince#256 both exist to close — so instead of
# "remember to add your seat", a new seat cannot declare itself until it appears here. The drift
# becomes a refusal at the moment of declaration, one PR wide, with the remedy obvious.
#
# ADDING A SEAT: add the name below, in a PR. That is the intended cost.
#
# AN UNKNOWN PREFIX STILL WAKES EVERY WATCH, deliberately and unchanged. quince#88 measured the two
# directions on the architect seat: five losses of the watch came from NOT arming, none from arming
# when nothing needed it. A spurious wake costs one re-arm; a missed wake costs an unwatched queue,
# which is what quince#62, quince#71 and the 6h29m outage were all about. The cheap direction is the
# noisy one.
#
# NOT a list of branch TOPICS. `journal/…`, `canon/…`, `report/…` are topic prefixes from before the
# convention was settled (quince#230) and must NOT be listed: a name here is treated as a runner, so
# listing `journal` would make quince-devlog#127 the property of a seat called `journal` and silence
# it everywhere that name is not local. That is fail-closed by accident, which is exactly what
# quince#265 rejected when it was proposed deliberately.

# The architect seat.
arch1

# Implementer runners. One host is meant to run several concurrently (quince#111), and the pool is
# reused: a name is released when its session is provably gone (quince#211), not burned forever.
r1
r2
r3
r4
